$string['promptdarkmode'] = 'CSS MUST support both light and dark mode: define light styles by default, use @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) for system preferences, and support explicit [data-bs-theme="dark"] and [data-bs-theme="light"] overrides where available. The HTML runs in an isolated iframe, so include its own theme styles rather than relying on attributes or CSS inherited from the parent Moodle page. Ensure readable backgrounds, text, borders and controls with sufficient contrast.';
$string['promptinstructions'] = 'Before generating any content, questions, HTML, CSS or JavaScript, or saving a draft, ASK THE TEACHER what they actually want to create in this activity, which topics to cover and what the learning objective is. Never infer the content from the activity title alone. Also ask whether they have documents, PDFs, images or other reference materials to use as a starting point; if they do, ask them to upload or share these files and review their contents before proceeding, without inventing information from unavailable files. Wait for the teacher\'s response and any materials they choose to provide before planning or creating anything. After that, ask any further questions needed to configure the activity, if applicable, and only then begin creating it. This activity will be EMBEDDED inside an EXISTING Moodle page that already displays the course name, activity title, navigation and theme interface. Use the Moodle address, course and course-module IDs, activity name and URL ONLY as technical context to locate and edit the activity through MCP tools. DO NOT reproduce these details in student-visible HTML and do not add a top metadata banner, institutional header, cover, breadcrumb, menu or navigation bar that duplicates the Moodle interface. DO NOT create ANY FOOTER in the HTML, even one that differs from the Moodle footer: do not use the <footer> tag or sections, divs or visual bars serving as a footer. Do not add credits, signatures, copyright notices, institutional closing messages, author names, support links or bottom navigation. If reference materials contain a footer, do not reproduce it. Before saving the draft, review and remove any footer; end the page immediately after the final educational section or last necessary interactive control. Do not create cards, labels, badges or text such as "Moodle", "Course", "Course ID", "Module ID" or the activity URL as metadata. Do not repeat the activity title at the top of the HTML just because it appears in the prompt. Start the embedded page directly with the requested educational content, using internal section headings only where pedagogically useful. Keep the HTML document complete, with its own styles for the isolated iframe, without duplicating the surrounding platform layout. The interactive document has an asynchronous window.MoodleMCP SDK exposed inside its sandbox. Use MoodleMCP.course.getActivities() and getNavigation() for allowed course links; MoodleMCP.completion.get() and complete() to respect the configured native completion rules; MoodleMCP.progress.load() and save({version, location, data}) to resume user progress; MoodleMCP.attempt.get()/start()/finish() and MoodleMCP.questions.get()/submit(responses, finish) for supported native question interactions; MoodleMCP.tracking.record({type, action, object, result}) for bounded learning events. These operations are asynchronous and can be denied by Moodle permissions. Never place session tokens, correct answers or server-side grading logic in the HTML. Do not assume completion is enabled or that a question type can run without its native Moodle JavaScript. The native assessment area remains available as a fallback. Instructions in this prompt are intended solely to guide page generation. DO NOT turn them into text, notices, labels, captions or other visible elements in the HTML.';
$string['promptpage'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit the EXISTING activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build an educational CONTENT PAGE, not a quiz or assessment. Create a complete, accessible, responsive HTML document organized into meaningful sections with headings, explanations, practical examples and helpful visual elements as appropriate, including CSS in <style> and JavaScript in <script> when useful. Do not create graded questions, scoring, answer keys or question bank attachments. Use the mod_mcp tools exposed by local_mcp to save a draft. After saving the draft, show me a preview and present two action buttons in the conversation: Approve and Reject (do not embed these buttons in the activity HTML). Publish the activity ONLY after I explicitly select Approve. If I select Reject, keep the draft unpublished and wait for my requested changes.';
$string['promptquiz'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Before creating any questions or content, ask the teacher how many questions to generate, whether to shuffle the question order, and whether each attempt should display all questions or a randomly selected subset. If a subset is requested, ask how many questions should appear in each attempt, and wait for the answers before proceeding. Build an INTERACTIVE ACTIVITY, such as a quiz, exercise, challenge or simulation, as a complete, accessible, responsive HTML document with CSS in <style> and JavaScript in <script> where needed. Use the mod_mcp tools exposed by local_mcp to save a draft. For graded questions, FIRST create or select native Moodle question bank questions, obtain their actual IDs and attach them to the activity. Use available native features to shuffle and select questions; never put answer keys, grades, grading logic or question selection logic in the HTML. If the available tools do not support a requested setting, explain the limitation rather than inventing functionality. After saving the draft, show me a preview and present two action buttons in the conversation: Approve and Reject (do not embed these buttons in the activity HTML). Publish the activity ONLY after I explicitly select Approve. If I select Reject, keep the draft unpublished and wait for my requested changes.';
